{"bill":{"id":"119-s-808","congress":119,"billType":"s","billNumber":"808","title":"Stop Russian Market Manipulation Act","introducedDate":"2025-02-27","latestActionDate":"2025-02-27","latestActionText":"Read twice and referred to the Committee on Finance.","policyArea":"Foreign Trade and International Finance","subjects":["Conflicts and wars","Europe","Metals","Russia","Trade restrictions","Ukraine"],"sponsorBioguideId":"D000618","cosponsorBioguideIds":["P000595","S001232","S001208","S001198","M001243"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"Stop Russian Market Manipulation Act This bill prohibits the importation of specified minerals from Russia. Specifically, the bill prohibits the importation of platinum, palladium, braggite, rhodium, ruthenium, nickel, cooper, or zinc if the mineral is (1) produced in Russia or by a Russian entity; or (2) determined to have been exchanged with, swapped for, or otherwise obtained in lieu of such a mineral in a manner designed to evade or circumvent this prohibition. The President may not waive this prohibition. The prohibition shall continue to apply until one year after the President certifies to Congress that the Russian government has ended all hostilities against Ukraine. If the Russian government resumes hostilities against Ukraine within three years of a certification, then the prohibition shall resume effect.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/808","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/s/BILLSTATUS-119s808.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:02.723Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-s-808","plainSummary":"This bill would ban the import of eight minerals—platinum, palladium, rhodium, ruthenium, nickel, copper, zinc, and braggite—if they are produced in Russia or by Russian entities. The ban would also cover minerals obtained through swaps or other arrangements designed to evade the restriction. The prohibition would remain in effect until one year after the President certifies that Russia has ended hostilities against Ukraine, and would resume if Russia restarts hostilities within three years.","industries":[{"industry":"mining","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly restricts imports of eight specific metals and minerals, affecting companies that source, process, or trade these materials."},{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Manufacturers that rely on platinum-group metals, nickel, copper, or zinc as inputs would face supply-chain changes and potentially higher costs."},{"industry":"defense","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Defense contractors use platinum-group metals and specialty alloys in aerospace and weapons systems, making supply sourcing relevant."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"77f26c0dd6a90bf6b370d98e","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:52:03.898Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would ban the importation of specified minerals from Russia.","whoItTouches":"Anyone who buys imported goods, and people who make goods here.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in prices, and what is made where. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-s-808","bioguideId":"S001198","cycle":2026,"score":0.009652179884638188,"matches":[{"industry":"manufacturing","donorAmount":47500,"donorShare":0.011086634829710363,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.003926516502189087},{"industry":"defense","donorAmount":62500,"donorShare":0.014587677407513634,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.0036469193518784086},{"industry":"mining","donorAmount":22500,"donorShare":0.005251563866704908,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.0020787440305706927}],"method